---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const rounds = [
  [1, 4, 1], [2, 9, 3], [3, 8, 6], [4, 12, 4], [5, 7, 6], [6, 5, 0],
]; // [round, incidents, with an official ruling] — aggregated from the exported DB, no referee or team columns
const maxInc = 12;
---

<Base
  title="Sofara: an incident library for Egyptian refereeing | Moaz Elkhashab"
  description="A searchable Arabic library of Egyptian Premier League refereeing incidents, built on the Referees Committee's own published rulings."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="head grid">
      <!-- TODO(moaz): rewrite the title and lead in your own voice. -->
      <h1>Sofara: an incident library for Egyptian refereeing</h1>
      <p class="lead col-text">
        Sofara organises 45 incidents from 51 Egyptian Premier League 2026/27 matches into a searchable Arabic
        library. Each verdict comes from the Referees Committee's own published post-round rulings. Built as a
        proposal for the EFA Referees Committee; submitted in Oct 2026.
      </p>
      <ul class="links col-wide">
        <li><a href="https://moazreda.github.io/sofara/">Live demo</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/MoazReda/sofara">Code</a></li>
      </ul>
    </header>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="gt">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="gt">Ground truth is the hard part</h2>
        <p>
          Public data cannot say whether a refereeing decision was correct. The only correct or incorrect labels are
          the Committee's own post-round videos, so I store the call and the card as two separate verdicts, and the
          tool never judges a decision itself. 20 of the 45 incidents have an official ruling.
        </p>
        <p>
          Of those 20, the Committee called the on-field decision correct in 16 and incorrect in 4, and judged the
          card correct in 17 and incorrect in 3. The VAR changed the decision in 4 incidents. 33 incidents link to
          the source video.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="col-wide rounds" aria-label="Incidents per round, and how many have an official ruling">
        <figcaption>Incidents per round, and how many have an official ruling (round 6 is one match, the derby).</figcaption>
        <ul>
          {rounds.map(([r, n, k]) => (
            <li>
              <span class="rl">Round {r}</span>
              <span class="bar"><i class="all" style={`width:${(n / maxInc) * 100}%`}></i><i class="ruled" style={`width:${(k / maxInc) * 100}%`}></i></span>
              <span class="rn">{k} of {n}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="rej">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="rej">What I rejected before writing code</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Synthetic referees.</strong> Planting “hidden skills” in fake referees makes any validation circular.
          Synthetic data appears only in labelled mock-ups.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A second-hand referee table.</strong> A betting site's table had duplicate names from
          transliteration, totals that did not equal yellow plus red, a negative red-card value, many referees with 1
          to 3 matches, and foreign referees mixed in.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Per-referee card rates.</strong> Referees given derbies and big matches show more cards, and public
          data cannot control for match difficulty, so these rates would mislead.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="data">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="data">Data and pipeline</h2>
        <p>
          Version-controlled CSVs go through strict loaders: a bad row fails the whole build and lists every problem.
          The result is a SQLite database where integrity is enforced in SQL, with CHECK, FOREIGN KEY and UNIQUE
          constraints and four triggers, not only in Python.
        </p>
        <p>
          It holds 109 officials, each with one ID. Names are never guessed, and every alias is recorded with its
          evidence. It also holds 52 expert opinions from 14 named sources, 11 of them independent experts with 47 opinions, published by 15
          outlets. Each opinion is summarised in my own words and links to where it was said.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="col-note">51 matches, 45 incidents, 109 officials, 52 opinions.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="stats">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="stats">Statistics that refuse to overclaim</h2>
        <p>
          Each disputed case gets a disagreement score (2 × minority ÷ opinions) and a Wilson 95% interval for the share
          who back the decision. An expert's agreement rate with the Committee appears only after 10 ruled cases. Fewer
          than 3 independent opinions is labelled “small sample”, with no verdict.
        </p>
        <p>The wording is always “agreement with the Committee”, never “accuracy”.</p>
        <p>
          Only 5 cases have two or more independent opinions, and the experts split in 4 of them. With a sample that
          small, the interval is the honest part of the output.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="search">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="search">Arabic search</h2>
        <p>
          The search is rule-based, not machine learning. It normalises Arabic spelling, matches whole words through
          common prefixes and suffixes, and maps 84 colloquial terms to structured fields. Every result says why it
          matched.
        </p>
        <p>
          The search is built never to miss a case: a test fails if any expected incident is absent, and it runs on a
          19-query gold set that I wrote myself, so it is a guard, not an independent score. Precision is reported for
          context only. The weakest query, “الكرة بيده”, returns many other handball cases (precision 0.125 at worst),
          and some of those extra hits may be relevant. A parity test checks that the page's JavaScript search returns
          the same cases as the Python search on all 19.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="eng">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="eng">Engineering</h2>
        <p>
          The core package uses only the Python standard library. 157 tests run in CI on Ubuntu and Windows with Python
          3.10 and 3.13, and the site deploys to GitHub Pages after every green run. 18 architecture decision records
          explain the choices above.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="limits">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="limits">Limits and next step</h2>
        <p>
          There is no trained model in Sofara. It is data engineering, statistics and search, and 45 incidents is a
          small sample. Next I plan to add semantic search with Arabic sentence embeddings and score it with Recall@k
          on the existing 19-query set.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    padding-top: 40px;
    row-gap: 24px;
  }
  .head h1 {
    grid-column: 1 / span 10;
    font-size: clamp(36px, 6.5vw, 64px);
    line-height: 1.02;
    max-width: 18ch;
  }
  .lead {
    font-size: 20px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .rounds figcaption {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: 14px;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }
  .rounds ul {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
    max-width: 640px;
  }
  .rounds li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 76px 1fr 64px;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    font-size: 14px;
  }
  .rl, .rn { color: var(--ink-soft); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .bar { position: relative; height: 12px; background: var(--panel); }
  .bar i { position: absolute; inset-block: 0; inset-inline-start: 0; display: block; }
  .bar .all { background: var(--rule); }
  .bar .ruled { background: var(--teal, #5FC9CF); }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .head h1 {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }
  }
</style>
